["三芳村",0,2006],["丸山町",0,2006],["千倉町",0,2006],["和田町",0,2006],["富山町",0,2006],["富浦町",0,2006],["白浜町",0,2006]],"12235":[["八日市場市",0,2006],["野栄町",0,2006]],"12236":[["佐原市",0,2006],["小見川町",0,2006],["山田町",0,2006],["栗源町",0,2006]],"12237":[["山武町",0,2006],["成東町",0,2006],["松尾町",0,2006],["蓮沼村",0,2006]],"12238":[["大原町",0,2005],["夷隅町",0,2005],["岬町",0,2005]],"12239":[["大網白里町",0,2013]],"12410":[["光町",0,2006],["横芝町",0,2006]]},"yurai":{"12":{"船橋市":"Legend says Yamato Takeru crossed the Ebi River on a bridge of boats — a \"funabashi\".","松戸市":"Theories include \"uma-sato\" (horse village, near the Koganemaki pastures) and \"matsu-sato\", a place of waiting — for Yamato Takeru's men or the ferry.","流山市":"Legend says a great chunk of Mount Akagi in Gunma broke off and floated down the Tone River to rest here — the \"drifted mountain\".","浦安市":"From the first village head Arai Jinzaemon's wish that the shore be peaceful (ura yasukare). In the Nihon Shoki, Izanagi calls Japan \"the land of Urayasu\", so it is also an old poetic name for the country.","野田市":"The leading theory credits the warrior Noda Umanosuke, who built a residence here — though he may instead have taken his name from the place.","木更津市":"The accepted story is Yamato Takeru's \"kimi sarazu\" — \"my lord, do not leave\". The earliest written form is 木佐良津, in a document of 1353.","成田市":"Proposed sources: \"naru-ta\", paddies where thunder rolls; \"nari-ta\", ripe paddies; or, in recent research, \"livelihood paddies\" cleared by settlers.","四街道市":"A signpost at the crossroads west of the station points four ways — Narita, Chiba, Tōgane, Tokyo — hence \"four roads\". In 1881 Fukushima Wakichi, first head of a private post office at the crossing, asked the prefecture to name it Yotsukaidō, and the name stuck.","茂原市":"From the Mobara estate (藻原荘, \"waterweed plain\") opened by the noble Fujiwara no Kuromaro, posted here in 774. The marshy name was rewritten 茂原 in the Edo period.","香取市":"Proposed origins of \"katori\" include a helmsman (kajitori), a sacred bird and \"deer-catching\". The spelling 香取 first appears as \"the sea of Katori\" in Book 11 of the Man'yōshū.","八街市":"When the shogunate's Kogane and Sakura horse pastures were cleared in early Meiji, the new settlements were numbered in order — Hatsutomi, Futawa, Misaki … — and the eighth was Yachimata.","袖ケ浦市":"In the Kojiki, Yamato Takeru's crossing of the bay was saved when his consort Oto-Tachibana-hime threw herself into the storm; her sleeve (sode) washed up on the Chiba shore, which became known poetically as Sodegaura.","旭市":"Said to come from a memorial poem by the Kyoto scholar Nonoguchi Takamasa at the 250th anniversary rites for Kiso Yoshimasa in 1844 — \"following the rising sun (asahi) out of Shinano\". Yoshimasa, descendant of the \"Rising-Sun General\" Kiso Yoshinaka, died here.","東金市":"From \"tokigane\": the ridge at Saifuku-ji looked like the head of a crested ibis (toki), and \"Toki-ga-mine\" became \"Tōgane\".","銚子市":"The wide Tone River narrows sharply at its mouth, pouring into the ocean like sake from a spouted chōshi flask. In ancient times the area was Misaki township."}},"kyu":{"12101":{"t":1,"k":100,"r":[["@jisha",100]]},"12102":{"t":1,"k":145,"r":[["@bakufu",145]]},"12103":{"t":2,"k":974,"r":[["@bakufu",974]]},"12104":{"t":97,"k":36110,"r":[["@hatamoto",13597],["Sakura Domain",10183],["Oyumi Domain",7355],["@bakufu",4401],["@other",574]],"p":1},"12202":{"t":40,"k":12640,"r":[["@hatamoto",5631],["Takasaki Domain",5624],["@bakufu",848],["Omigawa Domain",322],["@other",215]]},"12203":{"t":40,"k":14960,"r":[["@bakufu",11852],["@hatamoto",1552],["Tanaka Domain",1157],["@jisha",399]]},"12204":{"t":42,"k":14246,"r":[["@bakufu",11177],["@hatamoto",2995],["@jisha",75]]},"12205":{"t":92,"k":24823,"r":[["@bakufu",8341],["Mikami Domain",4667],["Maebashi Domain",3973],["Tateyama Domain",3361],["@other",4481]]},"12206":{"t":66,"k":27443,"r":[["@hatamoto",9949],["Maebashi Domain",5713],["@bakufu",4332],["Jōzai Domain",3240